---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import ArcLab from "../../components/ArcLab.astro";
import Excerpt from "../../components/Excerpt.astro";
import RoomNav from "../../components/RoomNav.astro";
import { gh } from "../../lib/openzfs";
import { room, roomNumber } from "../../lib/rooms";

const lang = "en";
const r = room("arc");
const A = "module/zfs/arc.c";
const L = {
  states: gh("include/sys/arc.h", 231, 238),
  key: gh("include/sys/arc_impl.h", 472, 475),
  counters: gh("include/sys/arc_impl.h", 162, 172),
  fractions: gh("include/sys/arc_impl.h", 1032, 1035),
  anon: gh(A, 5381, 5397),
  mintime: gh(A, 1318),
  hit: gh(A, 6025),
  miss: gh(A, 6204, 6208),
  hold: gh(A, 2334, 2338),
  release: gh(A, 2369),
  head: gh("module/zfs/multilist.c", 184),
  tail: gh(A, 3922, 3924),
  mfuHit: gh(A, 5450, 5459),
  mfuGhost: gh(A, 5460, 5472),
  hdrSize: gh(A, 1795, 1807),
  evictedState: gh(A, 3828, 3830),
  ghostDestroy: gh(A, 3820, 3823),
  wt: gh(A, 4499, 4501),
  evictImpl: gh(A, 4386, 4398),
  statics: gh(A, 4464, 4465),
  balance: gh(A, 459),
  init: gh(A, 8038, 8040),
  alloc: gh(A, 5251, 5252),
  overflowing: gh(A, 5099, 5100),
  recordsize: gh("module/zfs/dsl_dataset.c", 87, 91),
  evictCb: gh(A, 4876, 4877),
  evictLoop: gh(A, 4895, 4896),
  adapt: gh(A, 5076, 5081),
  cMax: gh(A, 5069, 5070),
  reduce: gh(A, 4714),
  blockSizes: gh("include/sys/fs/zfs.h", 1958, 1962),
  l2arcWhat: gh(A, 8217, 8222),
  feed: gh(A, 9771),
  feedHz: gh(A, 9791),
  feedTail: gh(A, 9530, 9538),
  l2cOnly: gh(A, 3805, 3819),
  l2cHit: gh(A, 5481, 5488),
  sublists: gh(A, 7518, 7543),
  randomSublist: gh(A, 4205, 4213),
  prefetchLife: gh(A, 3832, 3839),
  uncached: gh(A, 5473, 5480),
};
---

<Base
  lang={lang}
  roomId="arc"
  title="The ARC · Uberblok"
  description="How the ZFS ARC keeps a scan from wiping its cache, with MRU, MFU and ghost lists, and how ghost hits move its balance in OpenZFS 2.4.4: interactive, with the code."
>
  <article class="wrap">
    <header class="room-head">
      <p class="kicker">Room {roomNumber(r.id)}</p>
      <h1>{r.title.en}</h1>
      <div class="prose problem">
        <p class="lead">
          A cache in memory has to choose what to forget. Forget the least recently used block, as an LRU cache does, and
          one large read, a backup or a scan of a big file, pushes out everything that was being reused: every scanned block
          is more recent than the working set. Forget the least frequently used one, as an LFU cache does, and the blocks
          that were hot yesterday stay, however cold they are today.
        </p>
        <p>
          The ARC, the cache of ZFS, keeps the blocks seen once recently on one list, MRU, and the blocks seen at least
          twice on another, MFU, so a scan only passes through the first. And it remembers what it has just evicted: a
          <em>ghost</em> list keeps the header of each evicted block, without its data. A read that finds a ghost is a miss
          that a bigger list would have turned into a hit, and the ARC shifts its balance towards that list.
        </p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="lab">
      <h2 id="lab"><span class="n">1</span> Read, scan, read again</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The cache below holds 16 blocks of 128 KiB: that is its target size, <code>arc_c</code>. It opens with a hot
          set, H1 to H6, read twice a second apart, so all six are in MFU. Each list runs from its head, the most recent,
          to its tail, which is evicted first. Read a new block, scan twelve new ones, read the hot set again, or select any
          block to read it, cached, ghost or forgotten. A clock moves one second before each action, or 10 ms if you choose.
        </p>
        <p>
          Try a scan first: it passes through MRU, and the hot set stays. Then read a block from the MRU ghost list and
          watch <code>arc_pd</code> and the MRU target rise. Read a few scan blocks again to promote them, read new blocks
          until MFU gives up a hot block, and read that one back from the MFU ghost list: the balance moves the other way.
          Last, at 10 ms, read the same new block twice.
        </p>
      </div>
      <ArcLab lang={lang} />
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="depths">
      <h2 id="depths">The idea, at three depths</h2>

      <div class="depth">
        <h3>In one sentence</h3>
        <p class="lead prose">
          The ARC keeps blocks seen once apart from blocks seen twice, remembers the headers of what it evicted, and when
          a read finds one of those ghosts it gives more room to the list that would have kept the block.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="depth">
        <h3>In one paragraph</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          A new block enters MRU. A second read more than <code>ARC_MINTIME</code>, about 62 ms, after the first promotes
          it to MFU; re-reads within one burst count as one use. When the cache is over <code>arc_c</code>, an eviction
          pass takes blocks from the tail of MRU if MRU is above its share, and from the tail of MFU otherwise. An evicted
          block loses its data, but its header moves to the matching ghost list, which may hold half of what the other
          lists hold. Reading a ghost is a miss: the block is read from disk again, goes to MFU, and its size is counted as
          a ghost hit. There is no <code>arc_p</code> any more in OpenZFS 2.4.4. The shares are fixed-point fractions,
          <code>arc_pd</code> for the MRU share of data, <code>arc_pm</code> for metadata and <code>arc_meta</code> between
          the two, and the next eviction pass moves them with the ghost hits it finds: up for hits in the MRU ghost list,
          down for hits in the MFU ghost list, by at most about 1/32 at a time.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="depth">
        <h3>In detail</h3>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            <strong>States and headers.</strong> A block in the ARC has a header, <code>arc_buf_hdr_t</code>, keyed by
            <a href={L.key}>the block’s DVA and birth txg</a>, and the header is in <a href={L.states}>one of seven states</a>:
            anon, MRU, MRU ghost, MFU, MFU ghost, L2C only and uncached. The figure draws four of them, as lists. The header
            records its state, <a href={L.counters}>the time of its last access, <code>b_arc_access</code>, and hit counters</a>.
            A new block goes from anon <a href={L.anon}>to MRU</a>.
          </p>
          <p><strong>Seen once, seen twice.</strong> A hit on an MRU block:</p>
        </div>
        <Excerpt
          lang={lang}
          path={A}
          from={5398}
          to={5428}
          symbol="arc_access"
          notes={[
            {
              from: 5403,
              to: 5406,
              text: "A hit on a block whose read is still in flight only refreshes its time. In the figure every read completes at once.",
            },
            { from: 5407, to: 5408, text: "The hit is counted on the header." },
            {
              from: 5414,
              to: 5417,
              text: "A block first read by prefetch is not promoted by the demand read that follows. The figure has no prefetch.",
            },
            {
              from: 5423,
              to: 5428,
              text: "More than <code>ARC_MINTIME</code> after <code>b_arc_access</code>: to MFU, and the time is refreshed. Otherwise nothing moves, not even <code>b_arc_access</code>, so a series of re-reads less than 62 ms apart is measured from the first one. The test is strict: exactly 62 ms does not promote.",
            },
          ]}
        />
        <div class="prose after">
          <p>
            <code>ARC_MINTIME</code> is <a href={L.mintime}><code>hz &gt;&gt; 4</code>, “62 ms”</a>. A hit on an MFU block
            <a href={L.mfuHit}>stays in MFU</a> and refreshes <code>b_arc_access</code>.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Head and tail.</strong> <code>arc_read</code> calls <code>arc_access</code> <a href={L.hit}>on a hit</a>,
            and on a miss <a href={L.miss}>before the data is allocated</a>. While a reader holds a block, its header is
            <a href={L.hold}>off its list</a>; the last release <a href={L.release}>puts it back</a>, at
            <a href={L.head}>the head</a>, and eviction <a href={L.tail}>takes from the tail</a>. So each list is in order
            of last use. In the figure the reader lets go of each block at once.
          </p>
          <p><strong>Eviction keeps the header.</strong> When a block is evicted from MRU or MFU:</p>
        </div>
        <Excerpt
          lang={lang}
          path={A}
          from={3876}
          to={3891}
          symbol="arc_evict_hdr"
          notes={[
            { from: 3876, to: 3880, text: "The data is freed." },
            {
              from: 3882,
              text: `The header moves to the ghost state <a href="${L.evictedState}">chosen above</a>: MRU to MRU ghost, MFU to MFU ghost. In the figure it slides into the head of the ghost list, without data.`,
            },
            {
              from: 3884,
              to: 3889,
              text: "Only an uncached buffer’s header is destroyed. A ghost header stays in the hash table, so a later read of the same block finds it.",
            },
          ]}
        />
        <div class="prose after">
          <p><strong>Which list gives up a block.</strong> The eviction pass, after its metadata steps:</p>
        </div>
        <Excerpt
          lang={lang}
          path={A}
          from={4527}
          to={4556}
          symbol="arc_evict"
          notes={[
            {
              from: 4527,
              to: 4541,
              text: "Metadata first: MRU metadata down to its target, then MFU metadata down to the metadata share. The figure holds data blocks only, so these steps evict nothing.",
            },
            {
              from: 4543,
              to: 4550,
              text: "MRU data, down to its target <code>w = wt · arc_pd</code>, and no more than the cache is over <code>arc_c</code> (<code>asize - ac</code>). An MRU at or under its target gives up nothing.",
            },
            { from: 4552, to: 4556, text: "MFU data gives up whatever overshoot remains." },
          ]}
        />
        <div class="prose after">
          <p>
            <code>wt</code> is <a href={L.wt}>what can be evicted</a>, <code>arc_c</code> itself when every block in the cache
            is evictable, as in the figure. Each step evicts <a href={L.evictImpl}>at most what the list holds, and nothing for
            a negative amount</a>, from the tail, until it has evicted at least the amount asked. So MRU is not evicted down
            to its target in one go: it gives up its tail only while the cache is over <code>arc_c</code>. The figure’s
            first scan shows it: MRU keeps 10 blocks against a target of 8, because each new block puts the cache only one
            block over.
          </p>
          <p><strong>How long the ghosts last.</strong> At the end of the same pass:</p>
        </div>
        <Excerpt
          lang={lang}
          path={A}
          from={4558}
          to={4587}
          symbol="arc_evict"
          notes={[
            {
              from: 4569,
              to: 4572,
              text: "The MRU-data ghost list may hold half of the three other live lists. The figure has no metadata lists, so that is half of MFU.",
            },
            {
              from: 4579,
              to: 4582,
              text: "The MFU-data ghost list: half of MRU in the figure.",
            },
          ]}
        />
        <div class="prose after">
          <p>
            A ghost list is not the size of the cache. In the figure, with an empty MFU, the MRU ghost list holds nothing. A header trimmed
            from a ghost list <a href={L.ghostDestroy}>is destroyed</a>; the figure lists it as forgotten, and reading the
            block again is a new block to the ARC.
          </p>
          <p><strong>A ghost hit.</strong> Reading a block whose header is in the MRU ghost list:</p>
        </div>
        <Excerpt
          lang={lang}
          path={A}
          from={5429}
          to={5449}
          symbol="arc_access"
          notes={[
            {
              from: 5432,
              to: 5435,
              text: "“Would we have bigger MRU, it would be an MRU hit”: a ghost hit is a miss that a bigger list would have avoided. The data is read from disk again.",
            },
            {
              from: 5437,
              to: 5441,
              text: `Counted on the header, and the block’s size (<a href="${L.hdrSize}">its compressed size if compressed</a>) added to the MRU ghost list’s hit counter. The figure shows the counter under each ghost list.`,
            },
            {
              from: 5442,
              to: 5449,
              text: "Back into the cache in MFU, since this is its second use; in MRU if the block was a prefetch.",
            },
          ]}
        />
        <div class="prose after">
          <p>
            An MFU-ghost hit <a href={L.mfuGhost}>does the same with the MFU ghost list’s counter</a> and goes back to MFU.
            <code>arc_access</code> changes no target: it only adds up bytes. The eviction pass reads them.
          </p>
          <p><strong>From ghost hits to a balance.</strong> At the start of each pass:</p>
        </div>
        <Excerpt
          lang={lang}
          path={A}
          from={4479}
          to={4497}
          symbol="arc_evict"
          notes={[
            {
              from: 4480,
              to: 4491,
              text: `The ghost-hit bytes since the previous pass, four ways: MRU ghost and MFU ghost, data and metadata. The previous values <a href="${L.statics}">are kept between passes</a>.`,
            },
            {
              from: 4494,
              to: 4495,
              text: `<code>arc_meta</code>, the metadata share: up with metadata ghost hits, down with data ghost hits, the downward step divided by <code>zfs_arc_meta_balance / 100</code>, <a href="${L.balance}">5 by default</a>.`,
            },
            {
              from: 4496,
              text: "<code>arc_pd</code>, the MRU share of data: up with MRU-ghost data hits, down with MFU-ghost data hits. The figure’s gauge.",
            },
            { from: 4497, text: "<code>arc_pm</code>, the same for metadata." },
          ]}
        />
        <div class="prose after">
          <p>
            These three replace <code>arc_p</code>. They are <a href={L.fractions}>fractions of 2<sup>32</sup></a>, and
            start <a href={L.init}>at 1/4 for metadata and 1/2 for each MRU share</a>. The first argument after the
            fraction is the ghost target size of the previous pass, so a hit moves a fraction by about its size against
            the size of the ghost lists:
          </p>
        </div>
        <Excerpt
          lang={lang}
          path={A}
          from={4405}
          to={4442}
          symbol="arc_evict_adj"
          notes={[
            {
              from: 4409,
              to: 4410,
              text: "No change before there is any ghost target (the figure’s first pass) or without hits.",
            },
            {
              from: 4417,
              to: 4421,
              text: "Hits of 1/16 of the ghost total or more are scaled down to about 1/32 of it: one pass moves a fraction by at most about 1/32 of its range. In the figure, one ghost hit is enough to reach that limit.",
            },
            {
              from: 4424,
              to: 4426,
              text: "A shift chosen so the division keeps as many bits as possible.",
            },
            {
              from: 4428,
              to: 4435,
              text: "Near 0 or 1 (one side four times the other), the step towards that end is divided further: a fraction approaches its ends ever more slowly.",
            },
            {
              from: 4436,
              text: "The downward step divided by <code>balance / 100</code>: 1 for <code>arc_pd</code>.",
            },
          ]}
        />
        <div class="prose after">
          <p>
            The figure’s first ghost hit, worked through: <code>arc_pd</code> = 2<sup>31</sup>, one half; ghost targets of
            8 blocks, <code>total</code> = 2<sup>20</sup> bytes; one hit of 128 KiB, 2<sup>17</sup>. That is over 1/16 of the
            total, so it is scaled by 4 to 2<sup>15</sup>; the shift is 32; <code>up</code> = 2<sup>47</sup> / 2<sup>20</sup>
            = 2<sup>27</sup>, and <code>arc_pd</code> becomes 2<sup>31</sup> + 2<sup>27</sup>, 53.125%. The MRU target goes
            from 8 to 8.5 blocks. A hit in the MFU ghost list takes the same 2<sup>27</sup> away.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>When a pass runs.</strong> Not on a hit: a hit allocates nothing. Allocating the data of a miss
            <a href={L.alloc}>may wake the eviction thread</a>, once the cache is over <code>arc_c</code> by more than
            <a href={L.overflowing}><code>zfs_max_recordsize</code></a>, <a href={L.recordsize}>16 MiB on 64-bit systems</a>.
            The thread <a href={L.evictCb}>runs <code>arc_evict</code></a> and <a href={L.evictLoop}>goes on while it evicts
            something and the cache is still over <code>arc_c</code></a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong><code>arc_c</code> itself</strong> is another control. It grows <a href={L.adapt}>by the size of each allocation, at least
            128 KiB, while the cache is within 32 MiB of it</a> (<a href={L.blockSizes}>twice the largest block size</a>), up to <a href={L.cMax}><code>arc_c_max</code></a>, and shrinks
            <a href={L.reduce}>under memory pressure</a>. Ghost hits move the shares of <code>arc_c</code>, never
            <code>arc_c</code>. The figure keeps it at its maximum.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="l2arc">
      <h2 id="l2arc">Coda: the L2ARC</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          A cache device can extend the ARC: in the words of its design comment, <a href={L.l2arcWhat}>“a cache layer
          in-between main memory and disk”</a>, on solid-state disks for instance. The same comment says how it is fed:
        </p>
      </div>
      <Excerpt
        lang={lang}
        path={A}
        from={8261}
        to={8274}
        symbol="Level 2 ARC"
        notes={[
          {
            from: 8261,
            to: 8264,
            text: "Eviction does not write to the cache device: it frees data and leaves headers on the ghost lists, as above.",
          },
          {
            from: 8266,
            to: 8270,
            text: "A feed thread copies blocks from the eviction end of MRU and MFU before they are evicted.",
          },
        ]}
      />
      <div class="prose after">
        <p>
          That thread, <a href={L.feed}><code>l2arc_feed_thread</code></a>, wakes <a href={L.feedHz}>about once a
          second</a> and reads the lists <a href={L.feedTail}>from their tails</a>. A ghost header whose block is on the
          cache device is not destroyed when trimmed: it stays, <a href={L.l2cOnly}>as L2C only</a>, and a read of it
          <a href={L.l2cHit}>goes back to MRU</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section simplified" aria-labelledby="simplified">
      <h2 id="simplified">What the figure leaves out</h2>
      <p class="prose muted">
        The figure runs the functions above over data blocks only, all of 128 KiB and uncompressed: the metadata lists are
        empty, so the metadata terms of the pass are zero, and <code>arc_meta</code> and <code>arc_pm</code>, which then
        change no data target, are not drawn. Each state is one list; the code spreads headers over
        <a href={L.sublists}>sublists by hash</a> and evicts them <a href={L.randomSublist}>in turn</a>, so its order is
        close to, not exactly, the order of last use. A pass runs right after each miss that puts the cache over
        <code>arc_c</code>, rather than in a thread woken 16 MiB later, which at the figure’s scale would be eight caches.
        <code>arc_c</code> is fixed, the clock is logical, and each block is released as soon as it is read. No prefetch
        (prefetched blocks also have <a href={L.prefetchLife}>a minimum lifetime</a>), no <a href={L.uncached}>uncached</a>
        or dirty buffers, no compressed or encrypted copies, no L2ARC.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
  <RoomNav lang={lang} roomId="arc" />
</Base>

